<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milceni</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Milceni were a West Slavic tribe living around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>, in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Their land, called Milsko, was conquered by the Germans in the 10th century and later contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. Together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusici">Lusici</a> they are ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Milceni were a Sorbian tribe belonging to the wider group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a>. Their land, Milzavia, spread over the upper basins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> and Black Elster rivers. Its settled core, on fertile loess soil, measured about 50 km from east to west and 20 km from north to south, bordered by swamps to the north, the Lausitzer Bergland to the south, the hills of Burkau near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamenz">Kamenz</a> to the west and the Besunzane to the east. The origin of the name is disputed: scholars have derived it from personal names, from a river, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> milites or from the Slavic word for sweet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Bavarian Geographer first mentioned them as the Milzane in the middle of the 9th century, with 30 civitates. The German king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> defeated them in 932 and demanded their conversion, with partial success. Margrave Ekkehard I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> subjugated them around 990 and joined their land to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, but in 1002 the Polish duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> and the lands up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>.</p><p>The Germans recaptured the region in 1031 and returned it to the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. In 1076 it was enfeoffed to Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vratislaus-ii-of-bohemia">Vratislaus II of Bohemia</a> and later became the Land of Budissin, known from the 15th century as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>. The Milceni were still named in the 12th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Roland mentions men from Misnes with bristles down their backs like hogs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Archaeology has found large fortifications and castles in the Milceni's land, some with outlying castles, which point to a central authority; such a seat is missing among the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusici">Lusici</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a>. The castle network was probably built in defence before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s campaign. Little is known of the Milceni's princes, though one may have been Dobremirus, a venerable elder mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg">Thietmar of Merseburg</a>, whose daughter Emnilda married Bolesław I. Their descendants are the Upper Sorbian-speaking Sorbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Milceni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The area around Bautzen, the land of the Slavic <b>Milceni</b>, belonged to the Margraviate of Meissen.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-upper-lusatia">Margraviate of Upper Lusatia</a></li></ul></section>
